# 06 — More Shapes

> **For the agent reading this:** Read `plans/03-UX-improvements` (the editor, the gizmo, picking, the marquee) and `plans/05-second-ux-improvements` (copy, mirror, the MCP instructions) first. The idea came from `plans/thinking-01-entities`. Until now the scene has had one shape, the box. This phase adds three:
> - **cylinders**: circles, ovals and regular polygons, as rooms or volumes;
> - **free-form shapes**: closed bezier outlines drawn with a pen, as rooms or volumes;
> - **lines**: open 3D bezier paths with a thickness, dashes and arrows, for annotations.
>
> A box or cylinder can be **converted to free-form**, and free-forms and lines have a **point editing** mode. The groundwork comes first: every closed shape reduces to a **footprint polygon**, and rendering, picking, the marquee, bounds and room walls all run from it, so each new shape adds only its own small cases. The agent gets all of it through **`draw_shapes`** (which replaces `draw_boxes`) and `update_nodes`, with no other new tools. The work is split into small increments (§11). Each one ends with the human ↔ agent loop still working and a browser checklist for Orlando. Add this phase's terms to `GLOSSARY.md` (marked "from 06") as they land. Log progress in a §14 at the end of this file, as 05 did.

---

## 1. Goal

One demo, end to end:

1. Orlando presses `C` and drags a round tower room. On the contextual bar he sets **sides** to 8, and it becomes an octagon with flat walls facing north, south, east and west. `Shift` while dragging a second one keeps it a circle, and without it he gets an oval arena.
2. He presses `P` and clicks out a cave: some corners, and some click-drags that pull out curves. Clicking the first point closes it, and it becomes a room with curved walls.
3. He selects a box room, clicks **Convert to free-form**, and double-clicks it. Its four corners show as points. He drags one out to make the room a trapezoid, clicks an edge to add a point, and pulls a handle to curve a wall.
4. He presses `L` and clicks a platform's top, then the ground 6 m away, dragging the second click to curve it: a jump arc. On the contextual bar he turns on **dashes** and an **end arrow** and makes the line thicker.
5. He asks the agent for *"a circular shrine room, 10 m across, with a crescent-shaped pool in it and a dashed path from the door to the altar"*. It's one `draw_shapes` call: a cylinder, a free-form volume and a line.

